James inquired why C++ does not allow silent conversion from `vector<Child*>` to `vector<Parent*>`. I explained that `std::vector<T>` is invariant, meaning that a vector of pointers to one type isn't considered a subtype of a vector of pointers to another type, which is essential for type safety. I provided an example illustrating potential undefined behavior. James then asked about defining a general-use function `ostream& streamInsertVector(vector<IPointerSerializable*>& vec, ostream& os, PointerTracker& tracker);`, and I confirmed that templates could be used, detailing the function's implementation along with design notes, including the utility of the `Alloc` parameter for custom memory allocation. James then sought clarification on what `Alloc` is, which I elaborated on, explaining its purpose in C++ vector definitions.
